  "date": "2022-12",
  "title": "Looking Back At 2022",
  "language": "en",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "heading": "January",
      "text": "Robot ANYmal can walk over hill and dale – thanks to its visual perception and sense of touch, made possible by artificial intelligence."
    },
    {
      "heading": "February",
      "text": "Drawing inspiration from a butterfly, ETH researchers create structural colours using a nano 3D printer ."
    },
    {
      "heading": "April",
      "text": "ETH spin-off Climeworks raises 600 million Swiss francs in a financing round, which it will use to build large CO 2 filter plants worldwide."
    },
    {
      "heading": "May",
      "text": "For the first time ever, a multidisciplinary research team successfully transplants a donor liver that had been treated in a machine for three days."
    },
    {
      "heading": "June",
      "text": "Breast cancer tumours metastasize mostly when people are asleep . This finding could greatly change future diagnoses and treatment."
    },
    {
      "heading": "July",
      "text": "With the help of satellite data , researchers at ETH Zurich develop a method to quantify the amount of carbon released from Arctic permafrost."
    },
    {
      "heading": "September",
      "text": "ETH Zurich opens a new research and teaching centre with a focus on exploring the origin and prevalence of life on Earth and beyond."
    },
    {
      "heading": "October",
      "text": "Mars has been generally considered a geologically dead planet . An international team of researchers led by ETH Zurich reports that seismic signals indicate vulcanism still plays an active role in shaping the Martian surface."
    },
    {
      "heading": "December",
      "text": "Researchers from ETH Zurich have developed a new transparent gold nanocoating that harnesses sunlight to heat the lenses of glasses, thereby preventing them from fogging in humid conditions."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Most read articles",
      "text": "The above selection is hand-picked by the editors. But which articles covering ETH research were read most often last year? Here are the top 3:\n\nThe ETH News editorial team wishes you merry christmas and a happy new year."
    }
  ],
